# Terms the pre-commit guard refuses to let into a commit.
#
# Copy this file to `forbidden-terms.txt` and fill in the real ones. That copy
# is gitignored and never committed, for the reason the terms exist at all: a
# committed list of words that must not appear in the repository would put
# every one of them in the repository. The pattern is the one
# `geb-members.csv` / `geb-members.csv.example` already sets.
#
# One term per line. Matching is case-insensitive and on whole words, so a
# three-letter term will not fire on a longer word that happens to contain it.
# Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored.
#
# What belongs here: the employer's name and any abbreviation of it, internal
# system names that identify it, and internal governance vocabulary that would
# name the organisation to a reader who recognised it.
#
# What does not: domain words the repository is written in. Segment names,
# region names and role names stay -- the rule is about the name, not the
# vocabulary.

ExampleCorp
EXCO
